# `actuarial::life_contingencies::qx_lx`

Recovers death probabilities from the lives of a table.

## Signature

```cpp
pluss::Series actuarial::life_contingencies::qx_lx(const pluss::Series &lx);
```

## What It Does

Returns {math}`q_k=1-l_{k+1}/l_k`: the complement of [`px_lx`](px_lx.md), with the same requirements on the table. It is what you apply to a published life table to get the probabilities a [`Mortality`](../mortality/Mortality.md) stores; [`from_lx`](../mortality/from_lx.md) does it in one step.

## Parameters

`lx`
: One-dimensional series of lives, non-increasing, with a positive first value.

## Returns

A series of `lx.numel() − 1` probabilities.

## Errors

Throws the same errors as [`px_lx`](px_lx.md), prefixed `qx_lx:`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: probabilities from a table

Lives at five ages.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  out.writeln(life::qx_lx(pluss::make_series({100000.0, 99000.0, 97812.0, 96347.0, 94516.0})).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 2: a table that dies out

The final year has probability one.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  out.writeln(life::qx_lx(pluss::make_series({100.0, 50.0, 0.0})).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3: the round trip

`lx_qx` and `qx_lx` are inverses.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(8));
  const pluss::Series qx = pluss::make_series({0.010, 0.012, 0.015, 0.019, 0.024});
  out.writeln(life::qx_lx(life::lx_qx(qx)).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 4: one age is no years

A table with a single value has no probabilities.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  out.writeln(life::qx_lx(pluss::make_series({100.0})).numel());
}
```

### Example 5: the errors

A negative life and a table that dies out early.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto report = [&](const auto &call) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      std::string text(e.what());
      if (const auto cut = text.find("Triggered"); cut != std::string::npos) text.erase(cut);
      while (!text.empty() && text.back() == '\n') text.pop_back();
      out.writeln(text);
    }
  };
  report([&] { (void)life::qx_lx(pluss::make_series({100.0, -5.0})); });
  report([&] { (void)life::qx_lx(pluss::make_series({100.0, 0.0, 0.0})); });
}
```

